you get "Invalid Subscription ID"
and you're redirected to /admin/subscriptions

strongly suggests that Express is treating:

archive

as the value of a dynamic route such as:

/admin/subscriptions/:id

So somewhere before your archive route, Express is probably matching:

GET /admin/subscriptions/archive

against:

router.get("/subscriptions/:id", ...)

Then your controller tries to validate:

req.params.id === "archive"

and, correctly, concludes:

Invalid Subscription ID
This is likely a route-order issue

We previously had the archive route:

router.get(
    "/subscriptions/archive",
    authMiddleware.isLoggedIn,
    roleMiddleware.allowRoles(
        ROLES.ADMIN,
        ROLES.SUPERUSER
    ),
    subscriptionController.archivedSubscriptions
);

But if you have a dynamic route like:

router.get(
    "/subscriptions/:id",
    ...
);

the archive route must come before it.

For example:

// =====================================
// Subscription Archive
// =====================================

router.get(
    "/subscriptions/archive",
    authMiddleware.isLoggedIn,
    roleMiddleware.allowRoles(
        ROLES.ADMIN,
        ROLES.SUPERUSER
    ),
    subscriptionController.archivedSubscriptions
);


// =====================================
// Dynamic Subscription Route
// =====================================

router.get(
    "/subscriptions/:id",
    ...
);

Express evaluates routes in declaration order.

So:

/subscriptions/archive
        ↓
archive route first
        ↓
archivedSubscriptions()

rather than:

/subscriptions/archive
        ↓
/subscriptions/:id
        ↓
id = "archive"
        ↓
