        "/admin/payments"
    );
}

So if the log really shows:

paymentId: 6a7c8e4bd6753dea3e59aec8

then this particular request should NOT produce "Invalid payment ID."

That changes our direction

The problem is very likely somewhere in the view/link that is generating another request, or we're seeing the flash from a different request.

Your controller's flow is:

/admin/payments/:id
       ↓
req.params.id
       ↓
ObjectId validation
       ↓
Payment.findById()
       ↓
res.render("admin/payments/paymentDetails")

And this line is also correct:

res.render(
    "admin/payments/paymentDetails",
    {
        ...
    }
);

assuming the file is:

views/
└── admin/
    └── payments/
        └── paymentDetails.ejs
Here's what I want to check next

Please send me the current paymentDetails.ejs, especially:

the top/header include
anywhere it displays the payment ID
any buttons/forms
any links back to payment details
any href="/admin/payments/..."
any JavaScript that redirects or submits a payment ID

Because I strongly suspect we have something like:

href="/admin/payments/<%= something %>"

where something isn't actually payment._id.

One tiny diagnostic we can also add

Immediately after your validation, temporarily add:

console.log(
    "ObjectId valid:",
    mongoose.Types.ObjectId.isValid(paymentId)
);

So the log becomes:

paymentId: 6a7c8e4bd6753dea3e59aec8
ObjectId valid: true

If it says true, then we have absolute confirmation that the "Invalid payment ID" flash is coming from somewhere else, not this validation block.

And notice something else

If the ID were valid but the database couldn't find the payment, your controller would say:

Payment not found.

—not:

Invalid payment ID.

So there are only two relevant possibilities here:

Another request is hitting this controller with a bad ID, or
Something in the payment details view is generating a second malformed request.

Given that you specifically suspect the views, send me paymentDetails.ejs now. I think we'll find it there.


