Yes, partner — now that I can see the actual paymentDetails.ejs, the view itself does not contain anything that would generate "Invalid payment ID" when the page initially loads.

There is, however, one thing I want us to verify before changing anything.

What I see

Your action URLs are correctly built from:

<%= payment._id %>

For example:

action="/admin/payments/<%= payment._id %>/complete"

and similarly for:

/fail
/abandon
/refund

Those are fine.

Your back button is also fine:

href="/admin/payments"

There is no link in this view that calls:

/admin/payments/<some-invalid-id>

during the initial page load.

But I spotted something important

Your first line is currently:

<% include("../../partials/header") %>

It should be:

<%- include("../../partials/header") %>

And the footer should likewise be:

<%- include("../../partials/footer") %>

That fixes the EJS include rendering, but it is not responsible for the "Invalid payment ID" flash.

Here's what I think is happening

Your controller redirects here only in three situations:

1. Invalid ID
"Invalid payment ID."
2. Valid ID, but payment doesn't exist
"Payment not found."
3. Something else fails
"Unable to load payment details."

Your actual message is:

Invalid payment ID

So we need to prove what mongoose.Types.ObjectId.isValid() is actually receiving.

Add this immediately after:

const paymentId = req.params.id;
console.log(
    "ObjectId validation:",
    mongoose.Types.ObjectId.isValid(paymentId)
);

So your debug section becomes:

const paymentId = req.params.id;

console.log("=================================");
console.log("PAYMENT DETAILS ROUTE HIT");
console.log(
    "req.originalUrl:",
    req.originalUrl
);
console.log(
    "req.params:",
    req.params
);
console.log(
    "paymentId:",
    paymentId
);
console.log(
    "ObjectId validation:",
    mongoose.Types.ObjectId.isValid(paymentId)
);
console.log("=================================");

Then create/open the payment again.

I expect the console to say:
PAYMENT DETAILS ROUTE HIT
req.params: { id: '6a7c8e4bd6753dea3e59aec8' }
paymentId: 6a7c8e4bd6753dea3e59aec8
ObjectId validation: true

If it says:

ObjectId validation: true

then this controller cannot be producing "Invalid payment ID" on that request.

And that would be a major clue: the flash message is probably left over from an earlier request.

Why that is quite possible

Remember your global flash middleware:

res.locals.error = req.flash("error");

Flash messages are stored in the session until they're read.

So suppose an earlier request produced:

Invalid payment ID.

Then another request successfully renders the payment details page.

If the flash wasn't consumed where expected, you can still see that message in your header.

That would make it look like the payment details page is failing, even though:

Payment created ✅
Payment found ✅
Payment details rendered ✅
One more thing: your EJS syntax

Because the code you pasted has been escaped by the composer, I'm assuming your actual file contains normal EJS syntax such as:

<%= payment.reference %>

and not the literal:

*<*%= ...

If the actual file has normal EJS syntax, we're fine.

So let's do this in the safest order

Don't change the payment controller yet.

Change the header include to:
<%- include("../../partials/header") %>
Change the footer include to:
<%- include("../../partials/footer") %>
Add:
console.log(
    "ObjectId validation:",
    mongoose.Types.ObjectId.isValid(paymentId)
);
Create/open the ₦5,000 payment again.

Then give me the resulting console output.

If it says true and you still see "Invalid payment ID", we'll know with confidence that the ID validation isn't the culprit, and we'll trace the flash message itself rather than damaging working payment code.

And partner, the fact that the payment is now correctly showing ₦5,000.00 is a very good sign — we should preserve that working part.

