Ahhh, partner — now we have the missing piece. And this is actually good news.

Your Subscription model already has a built-in default:

cardNumber: {
    type: String,
    enum: Object.values(SUBSCRIPTION_CARDS),
    default: SUBSCRIPTION_CARDS.CARD_ONE,
    required: true,
    trim: true
}

So we do NOT need to generate a card number at all.

Mongoose will automatically assign:

SUBSCRIPTION_CARDS.CARD_ONE

when a new subscription is created without cardNumber.

The actual bug

Your activateSubscription() is fighting against the model default.

You currently have:

if (!cardNumber) {

    throw new Error(
        "Subscription card number is required."
    );

}

That check happens before:

subscription = await Subscription.create({
    ...
    cardNumber
});

Therefore Mongoose never gets the opportunity to apply its default.

The clean fix

For a new subscription, remove this validation:

if (!cardNumber) {

    throw new Error(
        "Subscription card number is required."
    );

}

And change the creation section from:

cardNumber

to either simply omit it:

subscription =
    await Subscription.create({

        user,

        plan,

        status:
            SUBSCRIPTION_STATUS.ACTIVE,

        source,

        startedAt:
            subscriptionStartedAt,

        expiresAt:
            subscriptionExpiresAt,

        activatedBy,

        notes

    });

Mongoose will then automatically apply:

SUBSCRIPTION_CARDS.CARD_ONE
For an existing subscription

